use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/FormParticipation import FormParticipation
from ../TextField import TextField

export ui-component EditableText:
    description:
        Editable text looks like ordinary text, such as a heading or a name,
        until the user chooses to change it. It then turns into a text box in
        the same place, and turns back into text when they save or cancel.

    composes:
        - @{TextField}

    behaviors:
        - @{FormParticipation}

    reading:
        While not being edited it shows the saved text. If there is none, it
        shows a hint such as "Add a title" in its place, which is not a label.
        It can still be reached with the Tab key, and assistive tools read it
        out as something that can be edited.

    starting:
        The component chooses how editing starts: clicking or focusing the
        text, double-clicking it, or pressing a separate edit button. From the
        keyboard, focusing it or pressing Enter on it always works. The
        component can also start and stop editing itself.

    editing:
        The text box sits where the text was and is about the same size, so
        nothing around it jumps. It can grow with what's typed, up to a
        maximum width. Whether the text is all selected when editing starts is
        up to the component.

    saving:
        Pressing Enter, or moving focus away, saves the text and turns it back
        into plain text. The component is told each time the text changes
        while editing, and told again once when it is saved, so it can act
        only on the saved text. The component can choose that moving away
        does nothing, or cancels instead of saving.

    cancelling:
        Pressing Escape throws away the changes and shows the text as it was
        before editing started. The component is told the edit was cancelled.

    buttons:
        The component can show Save and Cancel buttons while editing, and an
        Edit button while not.

    focus:
        After saving or cancelling, focus goes back to the text, or to the
        Edit button if there is one, so the user doesn't lose their place.

    lines:
        By default it edits one line. A version for several lines uses a
        TextArea instead. There, Enter starts a new line and saving needs a
        key combination, which is up to the chosen library.

    disabled:
        Disabled or read-only editable text can't be switched into editing. It
        just shows its text.

    nonGoals:
        - No formatting, such as bold or links. That's a rich text editor
        - It doesn't decide what's valid. It only shows the error it's given
